1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,038
If the Moon suddenly stopped in its orbit tonight, it would fall.

2
00:00:04,038 --> 00:00:07,108
And you would have about four days and twenty hours.

3
00:00:07,108 --> 00:00:10,415
For most of that time, the sky would look almost normal.

4
00:00:10,415 --> 00:00:15,080
Then, in the final hours, the Moon would swell to twenty times its usual width.

5
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:19,037
And twenty two minutes before impact, it would start to tear apart.

6
00:00:19,737 --> 00:00:23,954
Today we run that countdown, day by day, and then minute by minute.

7
00:00:23,954 --> 00:00:31,380
After that, a slower and stranger way the Moon could come down, one that is happening right now around another planet.

8
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:32,639
And finally a twist.

9
00:00:32,639 --> 00:00:34,968
This crash has already happened once.

10
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:37,422
It is the reason we have a Moon at all.

11
00:00:38,122 --> 00:00:39,807
What If the Moon Crashed Into Earth?

12
00:00:40,508 --> 00:00:42,401
First, what exactly is falling.

13
00:00:42,401 --> 00:00:49,912
The Moon is about three thousand four hundred and seventy four kilometers wide, a bit more than a quarter of Earth's width.

14
00:00:49,912 --> 00:00:53,515
You would need about eighty one Moons to balance one Earth.

15
00:00:53,515 --> 00:00:58,157
It sits, on average, three hundred and eighty four thousand kilometers away.

16
00:00:58,157 --> 00:01:00,477
And here is the counterintuitive part.

17
00:01:00,477 --> 00:01:04,325
The Moon is already falling toward us, right now, all the time.

18
00:01:04,325 --> 00:01:09,088
It just moves sideways at about one kilometer per second, so it keeps missing.

19
00:01:09,788 --> 00:01:12,499
Isaac Newton was the first to see it this way.

20
00:01:12,499 --> 00:01:17,332
The force that pulls an apple to the ground is the same force that holds the Moon.

21
00:01:17,332 --> 00:01:21,929
The Moon is a falling apple that is moving sideways fast enough to never land.

22
00:01:21,929 --> 00:01:24,699
So to make it crash, you don't need to push it.

23
00:01:24,699 --> 00:01:26,172
You only need to stop it.

24
00:01:26,872 --> 00:01:29,309
So let's take that sideways speed away.

25
00:01:29,309 --> 00:01:29,871
Day zero.

26
00:01:29,871 --> 00:01:34,307
The Moon stops dead, and Earth's gravity starts pulling it straight in.

27
00:01:34,307 --> 00:01:36,618
But this far out, the pull is gentle.

28
00:01:36,618 --> 00:01:42,554
After one full day, by our calculation, the Moon has fallen only about ten thousand kilometers.

29
00:01:42,554 --> 00:01:45,240
That is less than three percent of the way.

30
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:46,571
Day three.

31
00:01:46,571 --> 00:01:49,031
Look up, and you might not even notice.

32
00:01:49,031 --> 00:01:53,572
The Moon is still about two hundred and eighty thousand kilometers away.

33
00:01:53,572 --> 00:01:56,157
It looks around a third wider than usual.

34
00:01:56,157 --> 00:01:57,671
Odd, but not terrifying.

35
00:01:57,671 --> 00:02:01,897
The problem is that a falling object never falls at a steady speed.

36
00:02:01,897 --> 00:02:03,284
It keeps accelerating.

37
00:02:03,984 --> 00:02:04,532
Day four.

38
00:02:04,532 --> 00:02:05,627
Now people notice.

39
00:02:05,627 --> 00:02:14,145
The Moon is about twice as wide as normal, and because it covers about four times as much sky, moonlit nights are about four times brighter.

40
00:02:14,145 --> 00:02:18,282
It is still more than a hundred and eighty thousand kilometers away.

41
00:02:18,282 --> 00:02:19,681
Nothing has broken yet.

42
00:02:19,681 --> 00:02:22,480
But there are now less than twenty hours left.

43
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,327
From here, the countdown speeds up.

44
00:02:25,327 --> 00:02:30,357
On day four, the Moon is about a hundred and eighty five thousand kilometers away.

45
00:02:30,357 --> 00:02:34,835
Half a day later, at day four and a half, it has crossed most of the gap.

46
00:02:34,835 --> 00:02:40,908
It is now a hundred and six thousand kilometers out, moving at more than two kilometers per second.

47
00:02:41,608 --> 00:02:42,843
Now the oceans notice.

48
00:02:42,843 --> 00:02:48,343
Tides come from the difference in the Moon's pull between the near side and the far side of Earth.

49
00:02:48,343 --> 00:02:51,261
That difference grows with the cube of the distance.

50
00:02:51,261 --> 00:02:55,751
Bring the Moon to half the distance, and the tidal pull is eight times stronger.

51
00:02:55,751 --> 00:03:00,016
At day four and a half, by our calculation, it is about fifty times today's.

52
00:03:00,016 --> 00:03:06,077
Coastal cities would see the sea pulled out and pushed back again and again as Earth turns beneath the Moon.

53
00:03:06,777 --> 00:03:08,218
And it isn't only water.

54
00:03:08,218 --> 00:03:09,119
Rock bends too.

55
00:03:09,119 --> 00:03:16,564
Jupiter's moon Io is squeezed this way by Jupiter's tides, and it is the most volcanically active world in the solar system.

56
00:03:16,564 --> 00:03:23,528
In the final hours, Earth's crust would be flexed the same way, with earthquakes and eruptions on a planetary scale.

57
00:03:23,528 --> 00:03:30,372
When the Moon reaches the danger line we'll see in a moment, the tidal pull is around nine thousand times today's.

58
00:03:31,072 --> 00:03:34,802
Here is the view from a coastal town in those final hours.

59
00:03:34,802 --> 00:03:37,439
The Moon grows to twice its normal width.

60
00:03:37,439 --> 00:03:38,468
Then five times.

61
00:03:38,468 --> 00:03:39,046
Then ten.

62
00:03:39,046 --> 00:03:48,886
When it is twenty times wider than tonight's Moon, covering a patch of sky about as wide as your fist held at arm's length, it crosses an invisible line.

63
00:03:49,585 --> 00:03:51,561
That line is called the Roche limit.

64
00:03:51,561 --> 00:03:58,750
Inside it, Earth's pull on the near side of the Moon is so much stronger than on the far side that it beats the Moon's own gravity.

65
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:03,689
For a body like the Moon, it sits around eighteen thousand kilometers from Earth's center.

66
00:04:03,689 --> 00:04:06,378
Cross it, and the Moon begins to be pulled apart.

67
00:04:06,378 --> 00:04:08,353
But in this ending, there's no time.

68
00:04:08,353 --> 00:04:11,865
The Moon crosses the line only twenty two minutes before impact.

69
00:04:11,865 --> 00:04:15,378
It arrives stretched and cracked, but still mostly in one piece.

70
00:04:16,077 --> 00:04:19,699
Now the last minutes, seen by someone standing right under it.

71
00:04:19,699 --> 00:04:24,663
Ten minutes before impact, the Moon is about thirty degrees wide, by our calculation.

72
00:04:24,663 --> 00:04:26,824
Five minutes out, forty five degrees.

73
00:04:26,824 --> 00:04:33,833
In the final minute, a cracked, glowing wall of rock fills half of the sky, falling at nearly ten kilometers per second.

74
00:04:33,833 --> 00:04:36,344
Nobody standing there would hear it coming.

75
00:04:36,344 --> 00:04:38,797
It would arrive faster than its own sound.

76
00:04:39,497 --> 00:04:39,938
Impact.

77
00:04:39,938 --> 00:04:43,145
The Moon hits at roughly ten kilometers per second.

78
00:04:43,145 --> 00:04:48,679
By our calculation, it releases about three and a half million trillion trillion joules.

79
00:04:48,679 --> 00:04:52,578
Numbers like that mean nothing, so let's put them on a ladder.

80
00:04:52,578 --> 00:04:57,735
The most powerful bomb ever tested, the Tsar Bomba, released about fifty megatons.

81
00:04:57,735 --> 00:05:03,206
The asteroid that ended the dinosaurs released around two million times more than that.

82
00:05:03,206 --> 00:05:08,174
Boiling every ocean on Earth would take nearly ten thousand dinosaur asteroids.

83
00:05:08,174 --> 00:05:08,991
And the Moon?

84
00:05:08,991 --> 00:05:11,444
About eight million dinosaur asteroids.

85
00:05:11,444 --> 00:05:15,532
Around a thousand times the energy needed to boil all the oceans.

86
00:05:15,532 --> 00:05:20,814
Roughly everything the Sun shines out, in every direction, for two and a half hours.

87
00:05:21,514 --> 00:05:22,958
Would Earth be destroyed?

88
00:05:22,958 --> 00:05:23,883
As a planet, no.

89
00:05:23,883 --> 00:05:29,024
Pulling Earth completely apart would take more than sixty times the energy of the impact.

90
00:05:29,024 --> 00:05:30,989
But the surface would not survive.

91
00:05:30,989 --> 00:05:36,361
The crust would melt, the oceans would boil away, and the sky would fill with vaporized rock.

92
00:05:36,361 --> 00:05:42,254
Earth would become a glowing ball of magma, something it hasn't been for more than four billion years.

93
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:45,258
And the Moon would simply be gone, mixed into Earth.

94
00:05:45,958 --> 00:05:47,689
But that's the fast ending.

95
00:05:47,689 --> 00:05:50,572
There's a slower one, and it's much stranger.

96
00:05:50,572 --> 00:05:56,725
Imagine the Moon isn't stopped, but slowly dragged inward, spiraling down over a very long time.

97
00:05:56,725 --> 00:05:59,160
Now it reaches the Roche limit gently.

98
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:00,698
And it never hits Earth.

99
00:06:00,698 --> 00:06:01,595
Not as a Moon.

100
00:06:02,295 --> 00:06:07,336
Tides pull it apart into a cloud of rubble, and that rubble spreads along its orbit.

101
00:06:07,336 --> 00:06:09,136
Earth gets rings, like Saturn.

102
00:06:09,136 --> 00:06:14,536
Some of the debris would rain down as meteors, but a ring could stay for a very long time.

103
00:06:14,536 --> 00:06:15,496
Science fiction?

104
00:06:15,496 --> 00:06:17,536
It's happening right now, at Mars.

105
00:06:17,536 --> 00:06:21,737
Its moon Phobos is spiraling inward by about two meters every century.

106
00:06:21,737 --> 00:06:27,917
In roughly thirty to fifty million years, Mars is expected to tear it apart, and Mars will have a ring.

107
00:06:28,617 --> 00:06:29,436
Now the twist.

108
00:06:29,436 --> 00:06:31,486
A crash between Earth and the Moon?

109
00:06:31,486 --> 00:06:33,242
In a way, it already happened.

110
00:06:33,242 --> 00:06:40,326
About four and a half billion years ago, a world roughly the size of Mars, nicknamed Theia, slammed into the young Earth.

111
00:06:40,326 --> 00:06:44,776
Molten rock was thrown into orbit, and that debris gathered into a new body.

112
00:06:44,776 --> 00:06:45,303
Our Moon.

113
00:06:45,303 --> 00:06:51,040
Simulations run by NASA and Durham University even suggest it may have formed in just a few hours.

114
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:53,265
The Moon didn't come to crash into us.

115
00:06:53,265 --> 00:06:54,729
It was born from a crash.

116
00:06:55,429 --> 00:06:59,405
The evidence is in the rocks the Apollo astronauts brought back.

117
00:06:59,405 --> 00:07:08,972
Moon rocks have almost exactly the same mix of oxygen isotopes as rocks on Earth, a chemical fingerprint that is different on Mars and on most meteorites.

118
00:07:08,972 --> 00:07:12,203
The Moon is made largely of the same stuff as Earth.

119
00:07:12,203 --> 00:07:17,359
Exactly what you would expect if it was splashed out of Earth in a giant collision.

120
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:20,113
So could the Moon ever fall back?

121
00:07:20,113 --> 00:07:20,300
No.

122
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:21,918
It's moving the other way.

123
00:07:21,918 --> 00:07:29,760
Since the Apollo missions, scientists have been firing lasers at mirrors left on the lunar surface and timing the reflections.

124
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:37,105
The Moon is drifting away by about three point eight centimeters every year, roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.

125
00:07:37,105 --> 00:07:40,777
The tides it raises on Earth are slowly nudging it outward.

126
00:07:40,777 --> 00:07:48,432
And the same tides act as a brake on Earth's spin, so our days are getting longer by around two milliseconds every century.

127
00:07:49,132 --> 00:07:50,593
So the Moon won't crash.

128
00:07:50,593 --> 00:07:58,629
It was born in a collision, it would need four days and twenty hours to fall, and instead it's leaving us, one fingernail at a time.

129
00:07:58,629 --> 00:08:01,734
Next time, a question that sounds simple and isn't.

130
00:08:01,734 --> 00:08:05,447
If there are billions of stars out there, why is space black?

131
00:08:05,447 --> 00:08:06,177
That's next.
